\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

We compare G-MFRM against seven baselines that address judge bias or contamination separately (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): Raw Average, MFRM-only (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}), Sequential (MFRM then contamination adjustment), CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, CyclicJudge+discard, DCR Factor \citep{dcr2025}, and Preference Leakage calibration \citep{preference_leakage2026}. All methods are evaluated by Kendall $\tau$ and Spearman $\rho$ against Chatbot Arena human rankings.

\paragraph{Synthetic validation.} On synthetic data ($N = 50$, 20 seeds), G-MFRM reduces ability RMSE by 44\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 10^{-6}$) and improves ranking accuracy ($\tau$: $0.922$ vs.\ $0.859$, Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$). Contamination susceptibility is recovered with Pearson $r = 0.81$.

\paragraph{Predictive fit.} On Arena-Hard, LOO cross-validation strongly favors G-MFRM over MFRM-only ($\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}_{\mathrm{LOO}} = -55.7$, SE $= 9.4$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$; \citealp{vehtari2017loo}), with stacking weight 1.0 and all Pareto $\hat{k} < 0.7$ (Appendix~\ref{app:loo_comparison}).

\paragraph{Exposure coverage and ranking accuracy.} Despite improved predictive fit, G-MFRM's ranking accuracy on real data depends on exposure estimation quality. At $N = 17$, the full item set (500 items) has only 46\% of items with non-zero exposure estimates; G-MFRM over-corrects on the remaining items, yielding $\Delta\tau = -0.044$ relative to the raw average ($\tau = 0.794$ vs.\ $0.838$). We trace this degradation through three item subsets with increasing exposure coverage:
%
\begin{itemize}
    \item Full set (500 items, 46\% $e_i > 0$): $\Delta\tau = -0.044$
    \item Filtered set (317 items, 73\% $e_i > 0$): $\Delta\tau = -0.015$
    \item Exposed-only (231 items, 100\% $e_i > 0$): $\Delta\tau = 0.000$
\end{itemize}
%
The monotonic decrease ($-0.044 \to -0.015 \to 0.000$) establishes that over-correction is entirely attributable to incomplete exposure coverage. Two control variants confirm the mechanism: MFRM-only on the filtered set yields $\Delta\tau = 0.000$, isolating the degradation to the contamination gating term; a contamination-only variant without severity modeling yields $\Delta\tau = -0.118$, demonstrating that naive linear contamination deduction without joint calibration is catastrophically harmful.

\paragraph{Contamination diagnostics.} G-MFRM's primary real-data contribution is calibrated per-model contamination estimation. The contamination susceptibility profile (Figure~\ref{fig:contamination_bars}) reveals a clear gradient by model age: older models show $\hat{c}_n > 4$ (GPT-3.5-Turbo: $5.60$; GPT-4-0613: $4.20$), while recent models show near-zero susceptibility (GPT-4o: $0.88$; GPT-4o-mini: $0.93$). MFRM-only estimation absorbs this contamination signal into ability estimates, inflating $\hat{\theta}$ for the most contaminated models by up to 0.79 logits (Figure~\ref{fig:bias_absorption}; Spearman $\rho = 1.0$ between $\hat{c}_n$ and $\Delta\theta$). All parameters achieve $\hat{R} \leq 1.03$ with ESS $\geq 174$ (Appendices~\ref{app:item_diagnostics} and~\ref{app:ppc}).

\paragraph{Judge severity and panel design.} Judge severity profiles span 1.34 logits, with proprietary judges scoring more strictly (Figure~\ref{fig:judge_severity}). Contamination--ability separation improves with severity diversity: panels with $k \geq 4$ judges covering the severity extremes provide the most reliable diagnostic output (Appendix~\ref{app:judge_count}).

