\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced G-MFRM, a Bayesian measurement model that jointly estimates model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility within a single inferential framework. The key theoretical contribution is formalizing the confounding between judge bias and benchmark contamination as omitted variable bias: omitting either facet produces a closed-form bias in the other, with direction and magnitude determined by the exposure--severity covariance (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

On Arena-Hard, G-MFRM provides calibrated per-model contamination diagnostics unavailable from existing methods: older models exhibit 4--6$\times$ higher susceptibility, and MFRM-only estimation inflates ability for the most contaminated models by up to 0.79 logits. Ranking accuracy depends monotonically on exposure estimation quality ($\Delta\tau$: $-0.044 \to -0.015 \to 0.000$ as coverage increases to 100\%), establishing that complete exposure coverage is necessary for safe contamination correction. Ablation confirms joint estimation as the dominant contributor: staged estimation doubles ability RMSE.

Two practical implications emerge. First, evaluation frameworks that treat judge bias and contamination as independent incur systematic errors; future LLM evaluation should model these facets jointly. Second, the coverage--degradation finding provides an actionable quality requirement for contamination-aware evaluation. Several directions remain open: higher-rank contamination decompositions for domain-specific effects, simulation-based recalibration of $c_n$ credible intervals (71\% coverage at nominal 95\%), and scaling to larger model populations and diverse benchmarks.

